     The app previously used cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1) as its default
     for everything. That curve overshoots its target by a measured 9.8%, and
     CSS applies an animation's timing function to *every keyframe interval* —
     so on a keyframe set that already bounces, the two compound. Elements
     ended up travelling ~41px through five direction reversals to move 0px.

     Rule: overshoot comes from --m-ease-pop and nothing else. Keyframe sets
     stay two-keyframe, so there is at most one reversal. Never put an
     overshooting curve on height, width, padding, colour, or opacity — those
     either clip their content or clamp, and read as a glitch either way. */
  --m-ease-out:  cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);    /* expo-out, 0% overshoot — the default */
  --m-ease-soft: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1);      /* state changes: colour, background */
  --m-ease-pop:  cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.3, 0.64, 1);  /* ~2.5% overshoot — budgeted accents only */

  --m-dur-micro: 120ms;   /* press, hover, toggle */
  --m-dur-ui:    200ms;   /* tab switch, indicator slide */
  --m-dur-fade:  180ms;   /* tier 1 entrance */
  --m-dur-enter: 280ms;   /* tier 2 entrance */
  --m-dur-pop:   300ms;   /* tier 3 entrance */
  --m-stagger:    28ms;   /* cascade step; cap at 6 steps (~168ms) */
}

